### FEATURE_PROCESS — the template surface
###
### Every request here is a real one against a running instance. Sign in first: the session cookie and
### the cross-site token are what the chain expects, and a request without them answers 401 for a
### reason that has nothing to do with the endpoint being right.
###
### Ports: the demo backend runs on 8081 (Tomcat9 owns 8080 on this machine).
###
### The probes are ordered as a person would meet them: author, look, draw an edge, refuse a cycle,
### edit, refuse what may not be edited. Paste an identifier from one response into the next — the
### step identifiers come back in the authoring response precisely so the edges can be drawn.

@host = http://localhost:8081
@owner = maria@atelier.ro
@manager = ionut@atelier.ro
@employee = andrei@atelier.ro
@password = a-long-enough-passphrase

### Sign in as somebody who may record how the business works (owner or manager).
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{owner}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### The work a step will reference. CONSTRAINT-TEMPLATE-FIRST-01: a step names a task template rather
### than describing itself, so the library entry has to exist before the process can be authored.
### Two calls, because creating and approving are TASKLIB_UC_03 and UC_04 and there is no third door.
# @name work
POST {{host}}/api/task-templates
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Pregătește echipamentul",
  "description": "laptop, acces, birou",
  "priority": "NORMAL",
  "estimatedHours": 4,
  "checklist": [],
  "submitForApproval": true
}

### Approving it. **No body at all** — the endpoint takes an optional draft, and `{}` is a draft whose
### title is blank, which the request validation refuses before the use case is reached.
POST {{host}}/api/task-templates/{{work.response.body.id}}/approval

### PROCESS-AUTHOR-TEMPLATE-01 — author a template. 201, with an identifier for every step.
### Each step names its work; there is no title here, and the server would ignore one.
POST {{host}}/api/process-templates
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Integrare angajat nou",
  "overview": "Cum integrăm un coleg nou în primele patru săptămâni.",
  "steps": [
    { "taskTemplateId": "{{work.response.body.id}}", "expectedDurationHours": 4 }
  ]
}

### Extension 2a — no steps. 400 REQUEST_INVALID: a template with no work is not a process.
POST {{host}}/api/process-templates
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Gol", "overview": "nimic de făcut", "steps": [] }

### Extension 2b — a step naming no work. 400 STEP_TASK_TEMPLATE_REQUIRED, and `details[0].field` is
### `steps[1].taskTemplateId` — the position, because a form has to highlight a row.
POST {{host}}/api/process-templates
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Recepție marfă",
  "overview": null,
  "steps": [
    { "taskTemplateId": "{{work.response.body.id}}", "expectedDurationHours": 1 },
    { "taskTemplateId": null, "expectedDurationHours": null }
  ]
}

### Extension 1a — the same active name again. 409 TEMPLATE_NAME_TAKEN.
POST {{host}}/api/process-templates
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Integrare angajat nou",
  "overview": "a doua oară",
  "steps": [{ "taskTemplateId": "{{work.response.body.id}}", "expectedDurationHours": null }]
}

### Where one piece of work is used — PROCESS's half of the traceability page. 200.
### The process templates that plan it and the runs that have cut it. A template nobody uses and one
### that does not exist answer alike, with two empty lists.
GET {{host}}/api/process-templates/using/{{work.response.body.id}}

### The library. 200. Whoever may author or instantiate may read it.
GET {{host}}/api/process-templates

### One template with its whole graph. 200. Paste the identifier from the authoring response.
@template = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
GET {{host}}/api/process-templates/{{template}}

### PROCESS-DEFINE-DEPENDENCIES-01 — the second step waits for the first. 200, graph returned.
@stepOne = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
@stepTwo = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

### Somebody active, for the described-run section at the foot of this file. Copy one out of
### GET /api/workspace/people.
@personId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
@stepThree = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
POST {{host}}/api/process-templates/{{template}}/dependencies
Content-Type: application/json

{ "dependentStepId": "{{stepTwo}}", "dependsOnStepId": "{{stepOne}}" }

### Extension 2c — the same edge again. 200, one edge, and no second event in `process_event`.
POST {{host}}/api/process-templates/{{template}}/dependencies
Content-Type: application/json

{ "dependentStepId": "{{stepTwo}}", "dependsOnStepId": "{{stepOne}}" }

### The third waits for the second, so the chain is one → two → three.
POST {{host}}/api/process-templates/{{template}}/dependencies
Content-Type: application/json

{ "dependentStepId": "{{stepThree}}", "dependsOnStepId": "{{stepTwo}}" }

### G1, over a transitive path — the first now waits for the third. 409 GRAPH_CYCLE, and `details`
### names every step of the cycle, because an author cannot see the graph they are being refused.
POST {{host}}/api/process-templates/{{template}}/dependencies
Content-Type: application/json

{ "dependentStepId": "{{stepOne}}", "dependsOnStepId": "{{stepThree}}" }

### Extension 2d — a step that waits for itself. 409 GRAPH_CYCLE, a cycle of length one.
POST {{host}}/api/process-templates/{{template}}/dependencies
Content-Type: application/json

{ "dependentStepId": "{{stepOne}}", "dependsOnStepId": "{{stepOne}}" }

### G4 — an edge into another template's step. 422 CROSS_TEMPLATE_EDGE. Paste a step of a second
### template into `dependsOnStepId`; a step that exists nowhere answers 422 UNKNOWN_STEP instead, and
### the two codes are different on purpose.
POST {{host}}/api/process-templates/{{template}}/dependencies
Content-Type: application/json

{ "dependentStepId": "{{stepTwo}}", "dependsOnStepId": "{{stepOne}}" }

### Take the edge back out. 200, graph returned.
DELETE {{host}}/api/process-templates/{{template}}/dependencies/{{stepThree}}/{{stepTwo}}

### ── ADR-004: the observed order, and the act that makes one of them real ─────────────────────────
###
### The node pipeline writes the order it *watched* as OBSERVED edges. They carry the confidence it
### measured, they draw on screen, and they block nothing — every step of a freshly composed process
### is startable. *A followed B eight times* is not *B waits for A*: it may be a dependency, or it may
### be that one person does content on Mondays, and an edge that blocks on a guess makes a step
### unreachable in every future run with nothing on screen explaining why.
###
### To see one: run the pipeline (`node-pipeline.http`), open the composed template, and read its
### edges — `kind` is OBSERVED and `confidence` is set. Then instantiate it and confirm **every step
### is assignable at once**.

### PROCESS-DEFINE-DEPENDENCIES-01 — a person decides an observed order must hold. 200. From here the
### edge blocks, appears in cycle checks, and behaves exactly as a hand-drawn one.
POST {{host}}/api/process-templates/{{template}}/dependencies/promote
Content-Type: application/json

{ "dependentStepId": "{{stepTwo}}", "dependsOnStepId": "{{stepOne}}" }

### Press it twice. 200 both times, and the second changes nothing — a card is a thing people press
### twice, and so is an edge that already blocks. An edge that was never observed answers the same
### way: the caller asked for a state, and there is nothing there to argue with.
POST {{host}}/api/process-templates/{{template}}/dependencies/promote
Content-Type: application/json

{ "dependentStepId": "{{stepTwo}}", "dependsOnStepId": "{{stepOne}}" }

### 409 GRAPH_CYCLE — promoting the reverse of an edge that already blocks. **The observation is left
### exactly as it was.** Both directions were genuinely seen; what is refused is the claim that both
### must always hold, which is a claim about the future rather than about the evidence.
POST {{host}}/api/process-templates/{{template}}/dependencies/promote
Content-Type: application/json

{ "dependentStepId": "{{stepOne}}", "dependsOnStepId": "{{stepTwo}}" }

### PROCESS-EDIT-TEMPLATE-01 — change the overview and drop the third step. 200. The dropped step's
### edges go with it in the same transaction, and **no running instance is affected**
### (DECISION-PROCESS-SNAPSHOT-01) — instantiate first and re-read the instance to see that.
PATCH {{host}}/api/process-templates/{{template}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "overview": "Varianta scurtă: două săptămâni.",
  "steps": [
    { "id": "{{stepOne}}", "taskTemplateId": "{{work.response.body.id}}", "expectedDurationHours": 4 },
    { "id": "{{stepTwo}}", "taskTemplateId": "{{work.response.body.id}}", "expectedDurationHours": 8 }
  ]
}

### A step with no identifier is added. 200, and the new step comes back with one.
PATCH {{host}}/api/process-templates/{{template}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "overview": "Varianta scurtă: două săptămâni.",
  "steps": [
    { "id": "{{stepOne}}", "taskTemplateId": "{{work.response.body.id}}", "expectedDurationHours": 4 },
    { "id": "{{stepTwo}}", "taskTemplateId": "{{work.response.body.id}}", "expectedDurationHours": 8 },
    { "taskTemplateId": "{{work.response.body.id}}", "expectedDurationHours": 1 }
  ]
}

### Extension 2b — remove every step. 422 TEMPLATE_NEEDS_A_STEP.
PATCH {{host}}/api/process-templates/{{template}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "overview": "nimic", "steps": [] }

### Sign in as a manager who authored nothing.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "{{manager}}", "password": "{{password}}" }

### Extension 2c — a manager editing somebody else's template. 403 NOT_THE_AUTHOR. The owner is
### exempt; a manager is not, and the bound is in the use case because it depends on the request.
PATCH {{host}}/api/process-templates/{{template}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "overview": "al meu acum",
  "steps": [{ "id": "{{stepOne}}", "taskTemplateId": "{{work.response.body.id}}", "expectedDurationHours": null }]
}

### Sign in as an employee.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "{{employee}}", "password": "{{password}}" }

### 403 NOT_PERMITTED — an employee neither authors processes nor reads the library.
GET {{host}}/api/process-templates

### 403 NOT_PERMITTED — nor authors one.
POST {{host}}/api/process-templates
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Al meu",
  "overview": null,
  "steps": [{ "taskTemplateId": "{{work.response.body.id}}", "expectedDurationHours": null }]
}

### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### PROCESS-INSTANTIATE-01 and PROCESS-VIEW-INSTANCE-01 — running one
### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Sign in as somebody who may start a run.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "{{owner}}", "password": "{{password}}" }

### Start a run and name who steers it. 201. Every entry step comes back REACHABLE and everything
### else PENDING, in one transaction with the event and the notices.
### `processOwnerId` may be an employee — it is a per-instance position, not a role.
@processOwner = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "templateId": "{{template}}",
  "name": "Integrare — Andrei Munteanu",
  "processOwnerId": "{{processOwner}}"
}

### Extension 1a — a Process Owner who does not exist or is deactivated. 422
### PROCESS_OWNER_NOT_ACTIVE, and no instance is created. An unknown identifier and a deactivated
### person answer alike, so nobody can learn which accounts exist by watching the refusal.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "templateId": "{{template}}",
  "name": "Fără cârmaci",
  "processOwnerId": "11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111"
}

### The runs this caller may see. 200, scoped from the reporting tree at the moment of the read.
### Defaults to the board, so this answers exactly what it answered before `population` existed.
GET {{host}}/api/process-instances

### The same read, naming the board explicitly. Identical to the request above.
GET {{host}}/api/process-instances?population=ON_THE_BOARD

### Every run this caller may see, including the ones somebody put away. 200.
### **What any figure must be computed over** (PROCESS-ARCHIVE-INSTANCE-01): archiving is a statement
### about a screen, so a total that moves when the board is tidied has rewritten history. Run this
### beside the one above with a run archived — the lists differ by exactly that run, and by nothing
### else. `population` widens which runs are answered, never whose.
GET {{host}}/api/process-instances?population=EVERY_RUN

### A population that does not exist. 400, not a silently different list — which is the whole reason
### the parameter is an enum on the wire rather than a boolean or a free string.
GET {{host}}/api/process-instances?population=EVERYTHING

### Where one run stands. 200: every step with its condition, the progress, `awaitingAssignment` as
### its own field, and the bottleneck **as a step**. Nothing here is keyed by a person.
###
### What the operations canvas draws each node from, added with CANVAS-VIEW-PROCESS-01. On an
### assigned step: `assigneeId` and `assigneeName` — who **TASK** says holds it now, resolved for
### this response and stored nowhere, so an erased person comes back with an empty name and reads as
### a former member; `deadline`; `atRisk` as the workspace's window stood at this read; and `phases`,
### time per kind. **There is no total in `phases` and there is not going to be one** — look for a
### `totalSeconds` and satisfy yourself it is absent (DECISION-PHASE-TIMER-01).
###
### `edges` is new beside `steps`: every dependency as `from`, `to` and `satisfied`. Close the step an
### edge waits on, through TASK's own endpoints, and re-read — `satisfied` flips to true. Only PROCESS
### knows what Closed means for a step, so the flag is decided here rather than by whoever draws it.
###
### The standing question for any canvas surface: **is any number on this response keyed to a
### person?** Read it and satisfy yourself the answer is no. The assignee is attribution on the step
### that holds the work; every count on the response counts steps.
@instance = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
GET {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instance}}

### The snapshot, by hand. Edit the template above — rename a step, remove one — then re-read the
### instance. Its steps and edges are unchanged (DECISION-PROCESS-SNAPSHOT-01). This is the
### guarantee that makes editing safe, and it is worth watching once rather than trusting.
GET {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instance}}

### Extension 2a — sign in as somebody the run does not concern, and ask for it. 404
### INSTANCE_NOT_FOUND, **byte-identical to the answer for an instance that does not exist**, so the
### refusal does not reveal that it does.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "elena@atelier.ro", "password": "{{password}}" }

### 404 — the run exists and this caller may not see it.
GET {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instance}}

### 404 — no such run at all. Compare the two responses; they are the same.
GET {{host}}/api/process-instances/99999999-9999-9999-9999-999999999999

### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### PROCESS-ASSIGN-REACHABLE-01 — the hinge
### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Sign in as the Process Owner. **This may be an employee** — the position is per-instance, and
### the endpoint admits them through the ownership half of its disjunction rather than a grant.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "ioana@atelier.ro", "password": "{{password}}" }

### Who this run's steps may be handed to — the list the assignment dialog's picker renders.
### **Computed for whoever started the run, not for the caller.** The created task names the
### instantiator as its creator, so TASK's subtree rule runs against them; a list computed for the
### Process Owner would offer people the next call refuses and hide people it would accept
### (decision row 211). Try it as Ioana against a run Ionuț started: Ionuț is on the list, and he is
### not in Ioana's own subtree.
###
### It carries the assignment endpoint's own permission check, so signing in as Andrei — who neither
### holds PROCESS_ASSIGN_STEP nor steers this run — answers 403 rather than a subtree he cannot use.
GET {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instance}}/assignable-people

### Hand a reachable step to somebody. 200, and the step comes back ASSIGNED with its task.
### The created task names **whoever started the run** as its creator, not the Process Owner, so
### TASK's subtree rule runs against the person who decided the work exists (decision row 206).
@instanceStep = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
@assignee = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
### The response is the whole instance, read back through the view rather than answered from the
### aggregate — so the step you have just handed to somebody comes back **with them on it**, with the
### deadline you sent and its at-risk answer. It used to come back naming nobody, and the browser
### writes this response straight into its cache.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instance}}/steps/{{instanceStep}}/assignment
Content-Type: application/json

{ "assigneeId": "{{assignee}}", "deadline": "2026-12-31T17:00:00Z" }

### Extension 2a — a step whose dependencies are not met. 409 STEP_NOT_REACHABLE, and `details`
### names each step it is still waiting on. No task is created.
@pendingStep = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instance}}/steps/{{pendingStep}}/assignment
Content-Type: application/json

{ "assigneeId": "{{assignee}}", "deadline": "2026-12-31T17:00:00Z" }

### Extension 2b — the same step again. 409 ILLEGAL_STEP_TRANSITION carrying the condition it was
### actually in, because "already assigned" and "already closed" send a person to different places.
### No second task exists.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instance}}/steps/{{instanceStep}}/assignment
Content-Type: application/json

{ "assigneeId": "{{assignee}}", "deadline": "2026-12-31T17:00:00Z" }

### ── The loop closing, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
### Carry the created task to Closed through TASK's own endpoints, then re-read the instance. The
### step that was waiting on it is REACHABLE and nobody touched PROCESS to make that happen.
###
### Sign in as the assignee first.
@task = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "elena@atelier.ro", "password": "{{password}}" }

### Accept, start, complete — ordinary TASK, nothing about processes.
POST {{host}}/api/tasks/{{task}}/accept

### 
POST {{host}}/api/tasks/{{task}}/start

### After this one the task is COMPLETED. **Re-read the instance now**: the next step is still
### PENDING, because the graph advances on Closed and never on Completed — completed work may still
### be returned for rework (DECISION-PROCESS-REACHABILITY-01).
POST {{host}}/api/tasks/{{task}}/complete
Content-Type: application/json

{ "note": "gata", "externalLink": null }

### Sign back in as the reviewer.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "{{owner}}", "password": "{{password}}" }

### 
POST {{host}}/api/tasks/{{task}}/approve
Content-Type: application/json

{ "score": 4, "comment": "bine" }

### And close it. **This** is the move the graph advances on.
POST {{host}}/api/tasks/{{task}}/close

### Re-read the run: the closed step is CLOSED, the one that waited is REACHABLE and sits in
### `awaitingAssignment`, and progress has moved. Close every step and the run reports COMPLETE
### with a duration.
GET {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instance}}

### Extension 5a — instead of closing, have the assignee decline. The step returns to REACHABLE with
### its task reference dropped, and the Process Owner is prompted again. Without it the step would
### sit assigned against a task nobody holds and the run would stall invisibly.
POST {{host}}/api/tasks/{{task}}/reject
Content-Type: application/json

{ "reason": "nu am acces la depozit" }

###############################################################################
### PROCESS-ADD-TASK-01, PROCESS-REMOVE-TASK-01, PROCESS-REORDER-TASKS-01
###
### A run holds the workspace's own tasks (DECISION-PROCESS-MEMBERSHIP-01), so it can be corrected
### while it runs rather than only abandoned. Paste a step identifier from the run above into
### {{step}} and a task identifier into {{task}} as you go.
###############################################################################

### Which of my tasks could go in. Scope is TASK's answer; the subtraction — what a run already
### holds — is PROCESS's. An empty list has two causes and the screen tells them apart: you can see
### no tasks at all, or everything you can see is already in a run.
GET {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instance}}/attachable-tasks

### Attach one that already exists. Nothing about it changes: same assignee, same deadline, same
### state, same place in that person's queue. Read the task afterwards and only the two provenance
### columns differ.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instance}}/tasks
Content-Type: application/json

{ "taskId": "{{task}}", "dependsOnStepIds": [] }

### The same task again — 409 TASK_ALREADY_IN_A_PROCESS. A task in two runs would advance two graphs
### on one closure. The unique index refuses it as well, which is what settles a race the check
### cannot see.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instance}}/tasks
Content-Type: application/json

{ "taskId": "{{task}}", "dependsOnStepIds": [] }

### A task identifier naming nothing — 404 TASK_NOT_FOUND, byte for byte the same answer a task
### outside your scope gets. Sign in as {{employee}} and attach one of the owner's tasks to see it.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instance}}/tasks
Content-Type: application/json

{ "taskId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000", "dependsOnStepIds": [] }

### The other door: write a task straight into the run. This one *does* give somebody work, so it
### keeps every check TASK's create path makes — including the subtree rule, run against whoever
### started the run rather than whoever is steering it.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instance}}/tasks
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Comandă badge-ul",
  "description": "pentru acces la sediu",
  "assigneeId": "{{assignee}}",
  "priority": "HIGH",
  "dependsOnStepIds": ["{{step}}"]
}

### Both doors at once — 400 REQUEST_INVALID. The request has not said which act it means.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instance}}/tasks
Content-Type: application/json

{ "taskId": "{{task}}", "title": "și", "assigneeId": "{{assignee}}", "dependsOnStepIds": [] }

### Change the reading order. Every step of the run, every time: a partial order would leave two
### sharing a position. Nothing here touches a condition — what waits on what is the graph below.
PATCH {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instance}}/tasks/order
Content-Type: application/json

{ "stepIds": ["{{step}}"] }

### Say that one step waits for another, on a run that is already going. A cycle is refused with
### every step of it named. A step nobody has taken returns to pending; an assigned one is left
### alone, because somebody holds that task.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instance}}/dependencies
Content-Type: application/json

{ "dependentStepId": "{{step}}", "dependsOnStepId": "{{step}}" }

### And take it back out. If that was the last thing a pending step waited for, it becomes reachable
### in this same transaction and the Process Owner is told.
DELETE {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instance}}/dependencies/{{step}}/{{step}}

### Take a task out of the run. **The task survives** — same state, same assignee, same deadline,
### minus the process badge. Steps that waited on it open now. The last task of a run cannot be
### removed: 409 INSTANCE_NEEDS_A_TASK.
DELETE {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instance}}/tasks/{{step}}

### Read the task afterwards: it is still there, unchanged, and belongs to no run.
GET {{host}}/api/tasks/{{task}}

### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### PROCESS-START-FROM-TASKS-01 — a process out of work that already exists
### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Sign in as somebody who may start a run.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "{{owner}}", "password": "{{password}}" }

### Which tasks could go into a process at all — the picker the start dialog reads, for a run that
### does not exist yet. The same list `/{{instance}}/attachable-tasks` returns, scoped by TASK's own
### rule and holding only tasks that belong to no run, with no identifier to scope it by because
### there is nothing yet to refuse one against. `PROCESS_INSTANTIATE`, so an employee gets 403.
###
### **The literal segment beats `/{id}`**, which is why this is not read as an instance whose
### identifier is the word. Exercise this probe rather than the run-scoped one above: the two return
### the same shape, so calling the wrong one and getting a sensible answer proves nothing.
GET {{host}}/api/process-instances/attachable-tasks

### Start a process from two of them. 201, and `templateId` comes back **null**: this run was cut
### from nothing. Every step is ATTACHED with no definition, in the order given, showing each task's
### own title — no task is created, nobody is notified, and no dependency is drawn.
@taskOne = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
@taskTwo = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/from-tasks
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Închidere lunară august",
  "processOwnerId": "{{processOwner}}",
  "taskIds": ["{{taskOne}}", "{{taskTwo}}"]
}

### Extension 3a — no tasks at all. 400 REQUEST_INVALID. A run may not be emptied, so it may not be
### born empty.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/from-tasks
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Gol", "processOwnerId": "{{processOwner}}", "taskIds": [] }

### Extension 3c — a task that is already in a run. 409 TASK_ALREADY_IN_A_PROCESS, and no instance
### is created. Run the request above twice to see it.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/from-tasks
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Din nou", "processOwnerId": "{{processOwner}}", "taskIds": ["{{taskOne}}"] }

### Extension 3d — the same task named twice. The same 409, because it is the same thing: the second
### copy would be joining a run the first has already put it in.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/from-tasks
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "De două ori", "processOwnerId": "{{processOwner}}", "taskIds": ["{{taskTwo}}", "{{taskTwo}}"] }

### Extension 3b — a task outside the caller's scope, and one that does not exist. Both 404
### TASK_NOT_FOUND, byte for byte: an endpoint that says *that exists but is not yours* is a way to
### learn which identifiers name real work.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/from-tasks
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Nu e a mea", "processOwnerId": "{{processOwner}}", "taskIds": ["00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000"] }

### Extension 2a — a steerer who does not exist or is deactivated. 422 PROCESS_OWNER_NOT_ACTIVE, and
### nothing is written: the tasks keep no provenance.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/from-tasks
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Fără cârmaci",
  "processOwnerId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000",
  "taskIds": ["{{taskOne}}"]
}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
### PROCESS-RETIRE-TEMPLATE-01 and PROCESS-ABANDON-INSTANCE-01 — the process story gains an ending.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Take a template out of the library. 200. **Nothing is deleted**: run the instance read below
### afterwards and the run cut from it is still RUNNING with its provenance intact. As a manager who
### did not author it: 403 NOT_THE_AUTHOR — the permission passes at the door and the row-dependent
### rule refuses inside, which no annotation could express.
POST {{host}}/api/process-templates/REPLACE-WITH-A-TEMPLATE-id/retirement

### The same again. 409 TEMPLATE_IS_RETIRED, and no second event is appended — a log row for a change
### that did not happen is a false row.
POST {{host}}/api/process-templates/REPLACE-WITH-A-TEMPLATE-id/retirement

### Try to start it. 409 TEMPLATE_IS_RETIRED. The library list already hides it, so this is the door
### somebody with a stale page or a kept identifier reaches — which is exactly the case a screen
### filter cannot cover.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "templateId": "REPLACE-WITH-THE-RETIRED-TEMPLATE-id",
  "name": "Prea târziu",
  "processOwnerId": "REPLACE-WITH-A-PERSON-id"
}

### Author it again under the same name. 201. The unique index on the name is partial — `where active`
### — so retiring frees it, deliberately: a process written badly once must not lock its own name.
POST {{host}}/api/process-templates
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "REPLACE-WITH-THE-RETIRED-TEMPLATE-name",
  "overview": "A doua încercare",
  "steps": [{ "taskTemplateId": "{{work.response.body.id}}", "expectedDurationHours": 2 }]
}

### Stop a run that should not continue. 200. Read three things in the response: `state` is ABANDONED
### rather than COMPLETE — a run that was stopped is not a run that finished; `awaitingAssignment` is
### empty, because nothing is waiting to be given out in a run nobody is running; and
### `needingAttention` names the steps whose tasks are **still with somebody**.
###
### **PROCESS closed none of those tasks**, and that is the boundary rather than an omission
### (PROCESS_00 §4). End each one through TASK-OVERRIDE-01 — `POST /api/tasks/{id}/override` — on
### purpose and one at a time. That use case existing is what made this one buildable.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/REPLACE-WITH-A-RUNNING-INSTANCE-id/abandonment
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "reason": "Clientul a anulat comanda; nu mai continuăm producția."
}

### The same run again. 409 INSTANCE_NOT_RUNNING, no second event, and the first reason stands — the
### second attempt rewrites nothing.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/REPLACE-WITH-A-RUNNING-INSTANCE-id/abandonment
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "reason": "A doua oară."
}

### With nothing said. 400 REQUEST_INVALID. Everybody who had a task in the run reads this sentence,
### and a run stopped in silence was quietly forgotten with an extra step.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/REPLACE-WITH-A-RUNNING-INSTANCE-id/abandonment
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "reason": "   "
}

### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### CLOSING A RUN THAT SUCCEEDED — PROCESS-CLOSE-INSTANCE-01.
###
### **The hole this fills was a false statement about a real thing.** A run reaches COMPLETE on its own
### the moment its last step closes, so the ordinary ending needs no request. What had no door at all
### was the run that *succeeded* while a step nobody needs any more is still open — the client signed
### off in the meeting, the last two steps are moot. Until now the only way to end that run was
### `/abandonment`, so the way to record a success was to record it as a failure.
###
### V42 went to some length to keep those two facts apart in the very same table, and then left one of
### them with no way to be written. This is the counterpart.
### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Declare a running instance finished. 200.
###
### **Read three things in the response.** `state` is COMPLETE and `completedAt` is set — this run *did*
### finish, so every reading of *when did this complete* and every start-to-finish duration finds it.
### That is the asymmetry with abandonment, which deliberately leaves `completedAt` empty because an
### abandoned run did not complete. `closureNote` carries the sentence. And `needingAttention` lists
### the steps whose tasks are **still open**, because PROCESS closed none of them — it holds no port
### that mutates a task — and a person ends each one through TASK-OVERRIDE-01, on purpose and one at a
### time.
###
### That last field is the one worth checking by hand. Its gate read `!= ABANDONED` until this endpoint
### shipped, which would have made the *one* ending where somebody has decided the work is done the
### ending that never mentions the work that is not.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/REPLACE-WITH-A-RUNNING-INSTANCE-id/closure
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "note": "Clientul a semnat la ședință; ultimii doi pași nu mai sunt necesari."
}

### The same run again. 409 INSTANCE_NOT_RUNNING — it has already finished. The first note stands and
### no second event is appended.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/REPLACE-WITH-A-RUNNING-INSTANCE-id/closure
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "note": "A doua oară."
}

### With nothing said. 400 REQUEST_INVALID, naming the field `note` — not `reason`, which is the
### abandonment dialog's field and would highlight nothing on this form.
###
### Required for the reason the abandonment reason is, and arguably more so: everybody who held a task
### in this run reads it, and several of them are holding one that has just been declared unnecessary.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/REPLACE-WITH-A-RUNNING-INSTANCE-id/closure
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "note": "   "
}

### On a run that was abandoned. 409 — both endings are refused on a run that has already ended, and
### the database refuses the pair as well: `process_instance_ends_one_way` will not store a reason for
### stopping beside a note explaining that it finished. A record that contradicts itself is one no
### screen could render honestly.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/REPLACE-WITH-AN-ABANDONED-INSTANCE-id/closure
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "note": "Încercăm să o închidem ca reușită după ce a fost abandonată."
}

###############################################################################
### PROCESS-START-FROM-DESCRIPTIONS-01 — a run made out of work that does not exist yet
###############################################################################

### The third way a run begins. 201, with a step and a task per description.
###
### **One request because it must be one transaction.** Every step here could be done from a browser —
### create a task, create another, start a run over them — and DECISION-PROCESS-FROM-TASKS-01 examined
### that arrangement and refused it: *the person whose third one fails is left with a half-built process
### they did not ask for*. Five requests cannot be made atomic by the caller. One can.
###
### It is what the chat-assist form submits when somebody turns a conversation into a process, and it is
### an ordinary endpoint besides — anybody who may start a run may describe one.
###
### Each task is created through TASK's own create path, so the rule about whom the caller may give work
### to is applied by TASK, against the caller, exactly as on every other create. Requires
### PROCESS_INSTANTIATE, which the owner and managers hold and employees do not.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/from-descriptions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Aurora Coffee — brand strategy",
  "processOwnerId": "{{personId}}",
  "steps": [
    {"title": "Discovery workshop", "description": "What they believe they sell", "assigneeId": "{{personId}}",
     "deadline": null, "priority": "NORMAL"},
    {"title": "Competitor scan", "description": null, "assigneeId": "{{personId}}",
     "deadline": null, "priority": "HIGH"},
    {"title": "Positioning statement", "description": null, "assigneeId": "{{personId}}",
     "deadline": null, "priority": "NORMAL"}
  ]
}

### 403 — a step names somebody the caller may not direct, and **the whole run goes with it**.
###
### This is the request worth running twice: count the tasks before and after. A manager may give work
### to their own reports and not to somebody else's, so a plan whose second step reaches outside their
### subtree is refused — and the *first* step's task must not survive that refusal. Removing the
### transaction leaves exactly one orphan task behind, which is how the guarantee was proven rather than
### assumed.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/from-descriptions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "A plan she cannot wholly direct",
  "processOwnerId": "{{personId}}",
  "steps": [
    {"title": "Something for her own report", "description": null, "assigneeId": "{{personId}}",
     "deadline": null, "priority": "NORMAL"},
    {"title": "Something for somebody else's", "description": null,
     "assigneeId": "11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111", "deadline": null, "priority": "NORMAL"}
  ]
}

### 400 — a run with no work in it. A run may not be emptied, so it may not be born empty either: one
### invariant stated at both ends.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/from-descriptions
Content-Type: application/json

{"name": "Nothing at all", "processOwnerId": "{{personId}}", "steps": []}

### 422 — a run needs somebody who can steer it. An unknown identifier and a deactivated person answer
### alike, so a caller cannot learn which accounts exist by watching which refusal they get.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/from-descriptions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Steered by nobody",
  "processOwnerId": "11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111",
  "steps": [
    {"title": "Anything", "description": null, "assigneeId": "{{personId}}", "deadline": null,
     "priority": "NORMAL"}
  ]
}

### ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
### PROCESS-CATEGORISE-RUNS-01 — named groupings for runs, so a board of fifty is readable.
###
### **Only the named groupings are here.** *Runs one person holds entirely* and *runs whose work is
### shared* are derived where the board is drawn, from who holds each step. There is no endpoint for
### them and there should not be: a stored answer is wrong the moment somebody reassigns a step.
### ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### The groupings, and which run is in which. Requires PROCESS_VIEW_OWN.
### Both in one answer, because a board needs both to draw one thing and two round trips would let it
### render a filing against a grouping it has not heard of yet. The count on each is keyed to the
### grouping and never to a person (CANVAS_00 §5).
GET {{host}}/api/process-categories

### Name a grouping. 201 with its id. **Requires WORKSPACE_CONFIGURE**, granted to OWNER only in V19 —
### a manager is refused with 403, which is the whole point of the two permissions being different.
POST {{host}}/api/process-categories
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Client onboarding"
}

### The same name, differing only in case and surrounding space. **409 CATEGORY_NAME_TAKEN.**
### This one answered **500** when the endpoints shipped, because the refusal had no handler in
### PROCESS's advice — the server blaming itself for a rule it applied correctly. Run it.
POST {{host}}/api/process-categories
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "  client ONBOARDING "
}

### Rename one. 204. A grouping identifier from nowhere answers **404 CATEGORY_NOT_FOUND**, which was
### the second refusal with no handler.
PUT {{host}}/api/process-categories/REPLACE-WITH-A-CATEGORY-id
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Onboarding"
}

### File a run under a grouping. 204. **Requires PROCESS_EDIT_INSTANCE** — filing one run is an
### ordinary edit of that run, a different act from shaping the vocabulary everybody reads. A run
### belongs to at most one grouping: several would put it in three places on a board whose whole
### purpose is to make fifty runs readable.
PUT {{host}}/api/process-categories/runs/REPLACE-WITH-AN-INSTANCE-id
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "categoryId": "REPLACE-WITH-A-CATEGORY-id"
}

### Take it out again. `null` removes it; this replaces rather than adds.
PUT {{host}}/api/process-categories/runs/REPLACE-WITH-AN-INSTANCE-id
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "categoryId": null
}

### Remove a grouping. 204. **Its runs are not touched** — `on delete set null` brings them home to
### uncategorised, which is where they started. Check with the read above: the runs are still there.
DELETE {{host}}/api/process-categories/REPLACE-WITH-A-CATEGORY-id

### Put a finished run away, so the operations board stops carrying it. 204.
### **Requires PROCESS_ARCHIVE_INSTANCE** — a permission of its own rather than
### `PROCESS_EDIT_INSTANCE`, because editing a run changes it for the people working it while
### archiving removes it from *everybody's* board. Granted to OWNER and MANAGER in V59.
###
### **A statement about a screen, never about the work.** Nothing closes, nothing is deleted, no task
### is touched, and every figure the analysis and export surfaces compute counts this run exactly as
### it counted it before. Check with the run list above: it is gone from there and from nowhere else.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/REPLACE-WITH-AN-INSTANCE-id/archive

### The same call against a run that is still going. 409 INSTANCE_STILL_RUNNING, and a sentence
### rather than a constraint violation: archiving live work would hide it from the only screen that
### shows it. The schema refuses it too, so no other door can get this wrong quietly.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/REPLACE-WITH-A-RUNNING-INSTANCE-id/archive

### Put it back on the board. 204. Reversible on purpose — a board-tidying act that could not be
### undone is one nobody would dare use on a run they were unsure about. Restoring a run that is
### already on the board is not an error: the request names the state it wants, not a transition it
### believes is available, so two clicks and one click agree.
DELETE {{host}}/api/process-instances/REPLACE-WITH-AN-INSTANCE-id/archive

### ── SOP-METADATA-01: the optional step, and the run owner's decision ─────────────────────────────
###
### A step marked `optional` carries a `conditionNote` — the question somebody has to answer. Both are
### written when the template is authored; the run then carries **its own copy** of them, because
### `DECISION-PROCESS-SNAPSHOT-01` says a run is cut from a template and never linked to it again. A
### run cut in March where finance approval was mandatory still requires it in May after somebody
### marks that step optional.
POST {{host}}/api/process-templates
Content-Type: application/json

{"name":"Livrare campanie","overview":"Cum livrăm o campanie","steps":[
  {"taskTemplateId":"{{taskTemplateId}}","expectedDurationHours":4},
  {"taskTemplateId":"{{taskTemplateId2}}","expectedDurationHours":2,
   "optional":true,"conditionNote":"Is the value above 5,000?"},
  {"taskTemplateId":"{{taskTemplateId3}}","expectedDurationHours":8}]}

### The question is asked **when the step becomes reachable** — never at instantiation, where the
### answer is frequently unknown and a guess is then wrong for the rest of the run; and never of the
### assignee, because the step has no assignee until the question is answered. Read the run and look
### for `optional`, `conditionNote`, and `condition: "REACHABLE"` on the same step.
GET {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instanceId}}

### **Skip.** The step closes, so dependents release and a run whose remaining work was all optional
### completes — through the same transition a finished task runs, not a second one written beside it.
### `origin` is preserved and the skip is visible in history: a step skipped is a fact about the run
### rather than an absence from it.
###
### Two events are appended, and the pair is deliberate. `STEP_SKIPPED` records the judgement somebody
### made; `STEP_CLOSED` records what it did to the graph. A reader asking *why did this run finish
### without the finance step* needs the first; every consumer counting closures needs the second.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instanceId}}/steps/{{stepId}}/skip

### Idempotent. Send it twice: the second answers 200, changes nothing, and appends no second event,
### because a double-click must not be able to write one judgement into history twice.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instanceId}}/steps/{{stepId}}/skip

### 409 STEP_NOT_OPTIONAL — **the refusal that keeps the mechanism honest.** Without it, skip is a
### general-purpose way to make any step go away, and a run could reach Complete having silently
### dropped work its template requires. The way to remove required work is
### `DELETE /api/process-instances/{id}/tasks/{stepId}`, which looks like what it is.
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instanceId}}/steps/{{mandatoryStepId}}/skip

### 409 STEP_NOT_AWAITING_DECISION — the step is still PENDING (its dependencies are unmet, so nobody
### should be asked yet) or already ASSIGNED (somebody holds a task a skip would strand).
POST {{host}}/api/process-instances/{{instanceId}}/steps/{{pendingStepId}}/skip

### ── What a skipped step must never do to a figure ────────────────────────────────────────────────
###
### A skipped step has `closed_at` set — that is how the graph advances — so every query filtering on
### *closed* admits it. Three readings exclude it, and `V63`'s column comment carries the reasoning:
###
###   * `TemplateHistoryAdapter`'s edge query (`upstream.skipped_at is null`). Without it,
###     AI-INSIGHT-FALSE-DEPENDENCY-01 asks whether a dependent began before a step that never
###     happened finished — always true — and reports every edge into a skipped step as false.
###   * `TemplateUsesAdapter.cutIn` and `.countCutIn`. A skipped step is not a run of that work.
###
### Three further readings deliberately do **not** filter, and the reasons are in decision row 617:
### `planned` derives what the template *plans* and a skipped step was still planned; `max(closed_at)`
### is a real interaction at a real instant.
###
### Walk it: skip a step, then read the template analysis page and confirm no false-dependency finding
### appeared for the edge into it.
GET {{host}}/api/insights?subject_type=process_template&subject_id={{templateId}}
